    <exercise number="3" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Can both the products $$\lbrack C\rbrack.$$ and $$\lbrack A\rbrack\lbrack B\rbrack - \lbrack C\rbrack$$ be defined? If so, explain how; if not, explain why.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Yes, if the dimensions of $$A A$$ are $$m\times n m\times n$$ and the dimensions of $$B B$$ are $$n\times m, n\times m,$$ both products will be defined.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="4" has_solution="false">
